 \end{equation*}
 converges absolutely in the half plane $\Re(s)\geq \frac{1}{2}+\varepsilon$. Thus, the coefficient of $p^{-s}$ in the Euler product above decides the decomposition of the $L$-function $L_{l,j}(s)$. Thus, the lemma follows from the above lemma.

Assume $lj$ is even. We know that the degree of the $L$-function $L(s, {\rm{sym}}^{j}f)$ is $j+1$, so \begin{align*}
