sketch:
To prove \Cref{thm_existence}, the authors combine the following steps.
\begin{itemize}
\item \emph{Existence:} in \Cref{sec:esistenza_minimo_rhoc}, for all $c>0$ they prove existence of a minimizer $\rho_c\in X$ for $\mathcal{F}_c$.
\item \emph{Uniqueness/monotonicity/shape via a BVP:} in \Cref{sec:studio_bvp}, “above $\rho_0$, the unique stable catenary for $c=0$,” they show the minimizer $\rho_c$ is unique; they also “provide the monotonicity property with respect to the parameter $c$” and “characterize the shape of the minimizer $\rho_c$ solving a boundary value problem.” Concretely, by deriving the Euler–Lagrange equation they obtain that any minimizer solves
\[
\left\{
\begin{aligned}
&\varrho''=\frac{(\varrho^2-c)(1+(\varrho')^2)}{\varrho(\varrho^2+c)}\\
&\varrho(\pm h)=r.
\end{aligned}
\right.
\]
In \Cref{prop:proprieta_bvp} they compute explicit solution families (with a parameter $E=E(c)>0$ fixed by a transcendental equation from the boundary conditions), and although the transcendental equation may have multiple solutions (so “we do not have uniqueness of critical points”), in both regimes $c<r^2$ (\Cref{lemma:unicita_soluzioni_1}) and $c>r^2$ (\Cref{lemma:unicita_soluzioni_2}) they prove there is a unique critical point lying above $\rho_0$, yielding uniqueness of the minimizer.
\item \emph{Large-$c$ behavior:} in \Cref{sec:c_tinfty}, “by essentially a $\Gamma$-convergence procedure,” they characterize minimizers as $c\to\infty$, noting that “$c\mapsto \rho_c$ is an increasing function and $\rho_c\to \rho_\infty$ uniformly on $[-h,h]$,” and that this matches the expectation that $\mathcal{F}_c/c$ $\Gamma$-converges to $\mathcal{F}_\infty$.
\end{itemize}
A key technical point is that the energy density is “not coercive” and has “linear growth in $\varrho'$,” so they “relax $\mathcal{F}_c$ weakly in $W^{1,1}_{\rm loc}(-h,h)$” (\Cref{sec:relaxation}) and then prevent vanishing of $\varrho$ by splitting cases.
\begin{itemize}
\item If $c\in(0,r^2)$ (\Cref{sec:c<r2}), they show it is “energetically convenient to lie above $\sqrt c$,” proving $\mathcal{F}_c(\varrho\vee\sqrt c)\le\mathcal{F}_c(\varrho)$, hence they minimize the relaxed functional over $\{\varrho\in W^{1,1}_{\rm loc}(-h,h):\varrho\ge\sqrt c\}$. A first variation argument gives convexity and Sobolev boundary conditions; moreover (when \eqref{cond_catenaria} holds) they use \Cref{tilli1} to show $\mathcal{F}_c(\varrho\vee\rho_0)\le\mathcal{F}_c(\varrho)$, implying minimizers belong to $W^{1,1}(-h,h)$.
\item If $c>r^2$ (\Cref{sec:c>r^2}), they minimize the relaxed functional over $\{\varrho\in W^{1,1}_{\rm loc}(-h,h):\varrho\ge c_0\}$ with $c_0=\min\rho_0$; a first variation argument yields concavity and the boundary conditions. To conclude they show an upper bound is convenient: by \Cref{tilli2}, $\mathcal{F}_c(\varrho\wedge\rho_\infty)\le\mathcal{F}_c(\varrho)$, where $\rho_\infty$ is the unique minimizer (\Cref{lemma:esiste_minimo_Finfty}) of $\mathcal{F}_\infty(\varrho)=\int_{-h}^h \frac{\sqrt{1+(\varrho')^2}}{\varrho}\,dx$.
\end{itemize}
They also note the special case $c=r^2$ where “the constant function $\varrho=\sqrt c$ is the unique minimizer,” matching the statement in \Cref{thm_existence}.

