Before we state our main results, as in Remark~1~(ii), we define the generalized scaling for $\eqref{1.1}$: for $\lambda>0$, 
\begin{equation*}
  u_{\lambda}(x, t) := F^{-1}[\lambda^{-(2-\gamma)} F(u(\lambda x, \lambda^{2} t))]. 
\end{equation*}
Then, we see that if $u$ is a solution to $\eqref{1.1}$, then for all $\lambda>0$, $u_{\lambda}$ satisfies
\begin{equation*}
  \partial_{t} u_{\lambda} = \Delta u_{\lambda} + |x|^{-\gamma} f(u_{\lambda}) + \frac{|\nabla u_{\lambda}|^{2}}{f(u_{\lambda}) F(u_{\lambda})} (f'(u) F(u) - f'(u_{\lambda}) F(u_{\lambda})). 
\end{equation*}
Furthermore, the following invariance holds: 
\begin{equation*}
  \int_{\RN} \frac{1}{F(u(x, 0))^{\frac{N}{2-\gamma}}} \,dx = \int_{\RN} \frac{1}{F(u_{\lambda}(x, 0))^{\frac{N}{2-\gamma}}} \,dx, \quad \lambda>0. 
\end{equation*}
By this invariance, we expect that the optimal integrability condition of initial function $u_0$ for local existence of solutions to $\eqref{1.1}$ is given by
\begin{equation}\label{OIC}
