\begin{equation}\label{NLWE}
\begin{aligned}
&  \square u:=  \, \bigl( -\p_{x_0}^2 +\sum_{j=1}^n \p_{x_j}^2\bigr)u = Q(x,u,\nabla u)=q(x,u)((\p_{x_0}u)^2-|\nabla_{x'} u|^2).
 \end{aligned}
\end{equation}
 Such nonlinear potentials $Q(x,u,\nabla u)$ are called null forms after \cite{Chr,Kla,KlaMac,Sog}.  In fact we can show that one can recover $f(x) q(x,u)$ for any $f\in C_0^\infty({\mathbb R}^{n+1})$ and hence may assume that $q(x,u)$  is compactly supported in the variable $x$.

