Despite the strong empirical performance demonstrated in Section \ref{subsec:empirical}, the theoretical understanding of \Eqref{eq:obj} remains limited. The objective is nonsmooth and nonconvex, and finding a global solution is NP-hard in general \cite{gillis2018complexity}. Most existing landscape results apply only to the rank-one, noiseless regime (i.e., $k = r = 1$ and $S = 0$). In that setting, prior work shows that all local minima of \Eqref{eq:obj} are global \cite{josz2018theory,josz2022nonsmooth,guan2024ell_1,josz2025nonsmooth}, and that the ground-truth solutions form sharp local minima \cite{charisopoulos2021low}. Our aim, by contrast, is to provide optimality guarantees for the \emph{true} factorizations in the general-rank, noisy setting.

