We show that the linearized operator $L$ defined by \eqref{Ldef}--\eqref{ABDE} is closed and densely defined on $L^2(\mathbb{R}) \times L^2(\mathbb{R})$. We define the domain $\mathcal{H}$ of the operator $L$ as $\mathcal{H} :=\{ U =(v,u)^{\mathrm{tr}}: v \in H^1(\mathbb{R}), u \in H^2(\mathbb{R}) \}$.

\begin{lemma}
There exists a constant $C>0$ such that
\begin{equation} \label{2.1}
\lVert v \rVert_{H^1(\mathbb{R})} + \lVert u \rVert_{H^2(\mathbb{R})} \leq C \left( \lVert LU \rVert_{L^2(\mathbb{R})} +\lVert U \rVert_{L^2(\mathbb{R})} \right)
