Our proof follows the approach to constructing subsolutions as presented in Tao and Winkler 2025-JDE-TaoWinkler. More specifically, in establishing the main theorem, the paper first establishes a weak comparison principle. 

\begin{align}\label{extensibility}
\text { if } T_{\max }<\infty \text {, then } \limsup _{t \nearrow T_{\max }}\left(\|u(\cdot, t)\|_{L^{\infty}(\Omega)}+\|w(\cdot, t)\|_{L^{\infty}(\Omega)}\right)=\infty \text {. }
\end{align}
Moreover, $u, w>0$ in $\overline{\Omega} \times [0,T_{\max})$ and $\int_{\Omega} v(\cdot,t) dx=\int_{\Omega} z(\cdot,t) dx=0$ for all $t \in (0,T_{\max})$, as well as
\begin{align*}
\int_\Omega u(t) dx=\int_\Omega u_0 dx \ \ \text{and} \ \
\int_\Omega w(t) dx=\int_\Omega w_0  dx, \quad t \in (0,T_{\max}).
\end{align*}

If in addition $\Omega=B_R(0)$ for some $R>0$, and $\left(u_0, w_0\right)$ is a triplet of radially symmetric functions, then $u, v, w, z$ are all radially symmetric.
\end{prop}

It then constructs subsolutions which become singular within finite time; this yields finite-time blow-up. 

\begin{align}\label{eq2_14}
\max _{(s, t) \in[0, R^n] \times[0, t_1]}\{ X(s,t),Y(s,t)\}=Y(s_Y,t_1)=0.
