theorem.content:
\label{thm: main}
    Let $f:[0,1)\to \mathbb{R}$ be given by $f(x)=-\frac{1}{x^{a}}+\frac{1}{(1-x)^{a}}$, with $a>1$. For almost every symmetric interval exchange transformation $T$ on $[0,1)$, the skew product $T_{f}$ on $[0,1)\times\mathbb{R}$ given by
    \[
    T_{f}(x,y)=(T(x),y+f(x))
    \]
    has the following properties:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item[A)] for a.e. point $(x,y)\in [0,1)\times \mathbb{R}$, and every $L>0$ there exists $N=N(x,y,L)$ such that for every $n\ge N$ it holds that $T_{f}^n(x,y)\notin[0,1)\times [-L,L]$;
        \item[B)] for every open rectangle $D:=I_1\times I_2\subset [0,1)\times \mathbb{R}$, there exists an infinite increasing sequence $(q_n)_{n=1}^{\infty}$ such that $T^{q_n}_{f}(D)\cap D\neq\emptyset$.
    \end{itemize}
    In particular, by Property \textup{A}, $T_f$ is not ergodic with respect to $Leb_{[0,1)}\otimes Leb_{\mathbb{R}}$.

