expanded_sketch:
To prove the main theorem, the starting point is the representation formula
\begin{equation}\label{1}
\nu(x,t)= \int_{\Omega}p_{\gamma}^{\Omega} (x,z;t)u_0(z) d\gamma(z)
\end{equation}
where $p_\gamma^\Omega$ is the kernel of the parabolic Ornstein--Uhlenbeck operator (the fundamental solution of
\begin{equation}\label{H-PDE2}
\left\{
\begin{aligned}
&\Delta u -(x,\nabla u)=u_t, \quad\mbox{in} \ \Omega \times \{t>0\},  \\
&u=0,  \quad\mbox{on} \ \ \partial\Omega\times \{ t>0 \}, \\
&u(x,0)=u_0(x),\quad\mbox{on} \ \ \Omega \times \{ t=0 \},
\end{aligned}
\right.
\end{equation}
characterized by \eqref{parabolic-equation1}).

The strategy is then to obtain log-concavity of the kernel: since no explicit formula for $p_\gamma^\Omega$ is available on a generic domain, the authors follow \cite{BL76} and use the Trotter product formula “to deduce the log-concavity” (here, “applying the same strategy, we will obtain the log-concavity of the kernel of the Ornstein--Uhlenbeck parabolic operator”). We first prove the following theorem.
\begin{theorem}\label{Lemma-1}
Let $\Omega\subset \R^n$ be a bounded, convex open set. Then, for every $t>0$, the function
$$
(x,z)\, \rightarrow\,p^\Omega_\gamma (x,z;t) e^{-|z|^2/2},\quad(x,z)\in\Omega\times\Omega
$$
is log-concave.
\end{theorem}

Finally, this fact, along with the representation formula \eqref{1}, ensures the main theorem by a standard argument based on Pr\'ekopa's theorem.

