\begin{equation}\label{normalization}
\sum_{i=1}^r B_{i\bar i}=rI,
\qquad
\tr(B_{i\bar j})=r\,\delta_{ij}.
\end{equation}
which preserves positivity and the sign of \(\Phi\).” Under this normalization, “\(\Phi\) can be rewritten in terms of mixed discriminants of explicit third-order Hermitian matrices associated to \((B_{i\bar j})\).” With \(C(\xi)=\sum_{i,j}B_{i\bar j}\,\xi^i\overline{\xi^j}\), “a spherical moment identity expresses the relevant mixed-discriminant combinations as an average over \(\xi\in S^{5}\) of a symmetric polynomial in \(C(\xi)\).” Then “diagonalizing \(C(\xi)\) reduces the integrand to an inequality for symmetric polynomials of the eigenvalues of \(C(\xi)\), which follows from a sharp Schur inequality,” and “This yields \(\Phi>0\).” Finally, they say “the equivalence of Finski's problem with Griffiths' question in the \((3,3)\)-case translates \(\Phi>0\) into weak positivity of all degree-\(3\) Schur forms,” and the later claim follows by restriction to rank-\(3\) subbundles together with the principal-minor formula
\begin{equation}\label{eqn6}
c_3(E,h^E)
=\left(\tfrac{\sqrt{-1}}{2\pi}\right)^3
\sum_{1\le i_1<i_2<i_3\le r}
