expanded_sketch:
The proof of the main theorem is described as “a modification of our earlier work \cite{MR4201982},” exploiting that “the Fokker-Planck equation has a distinguished time variable, which we exploit to handle the non-compact setting,” and proceeds via “a parametrix construction.” In the case $B=I$, they:

1. “Start by pulling the Fokker-Planck equation back by the flow along the first-order terms in
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:Qdef}
Q=H_p
+\gamma\sum_{j=1}^J \Im(\bar\ell_j\nabla_x\ell_j)\cdot\partial_\xi
+\frac{\gamma h}2\sum_{j=1}^J (\nabla_x\ell_j\cdot\partial_\xi)(\nabla_x\bar\ell_j\cdot\partial_\xi).
\end{equation}
” In this pullback, “the leading order terms in the pullback of $\partial_t-Q$ are then $\partial_t-(\partial_\xi+t\partial_x)^2$” (in subelliptic order).

2. “Make a linear change of variables” to rewrite this as
\[
\partial_t-\tfrac 12\xi\cdot\partial_x-(\partial_\xi+\tfrac 12 t\partial_x)^2,
\]
which is “a H\"ormander type operator involving left-invariant vector fields on a step 2 nilpotent Lie group.” They “see that the fundamental solution on this Lie group can be written explicitly in terms of the Gaussian heat kernel.”

3. This explicit fundamental solution “produces the leading term in a left/right inverse for $\partial_t-Q$,” and then they “use heat kernel iteration steps to produce a left or right inverse to any desired order,” where “composition of kernels is expressed as convolution in the group product.”

4. For global estimates, they note “care needs to be taken” because pulling back derivatives yields “a frame whose second derivatives in $t$ increase unboundedly with $\xi$ due to the $\xi\cdot\partial_x$ term in the flow.” Hence “to obtain global estimates we therefore can use only the first few derivatives in $t$,” achieved by “using the heat kernel form of the fundamental solution rather than the pseudodifferential representation used in \cite{MR4201982}.”

They also indicate the role of the parametrix in the bounds: “Estimate
\begin{multline}\label{eqn:localest}
\sup_{0\le t\le \infty}\,\sum_{|\alpha|+3|\beta|\le 3N}
\|(\eps\partial_\xi)^\alpha(\eps\partial_x)^\beta u(t,\cdot)\|_{L^p(\R^n)}\\
\le 
C_N\sum_{|\alpha|+3|\beta|\le 3N}\|(\eps\partial_\xi)^\alpha(\eps\partial_x)^\beta u_0\|_{L^p(\R^n)}.
\end{multline}
is the result of the parametrix mapping properties,” with the $\alpha,\beta$ counting reflecting “the nonisotropic subelliptic calculus associated to $Q$,” while
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:globalest}
\sup_{T\le t\le \infty}\,\sum_{|\alpha|+3|\beta|\le N}
\|(\eps\partial_\xi)^\alpha(\eps\partial_x)^\beta u(t,\cdot)\|_{L^p(\R^n)}
\le 
C_{N,T}\|u_0\|_{L^p(\R^n)},
\end{equation}
“captures the smoothing effect of $e^{tQ}$ for $t>0$.”

