They “prove To prove the main theorem, … using a mixture between Kenig-Ponce-Vega stability type estimates, and the numerical properties ensured by the hypotheses (H1)-(H2)-(H3),” following “the method introduced in \cite{ACFMV24}.” A main complication is “to establish the correct functional setting to prove stability estimates,” since “the local well-posedness theory is not fully adapted” to the present problem.

The “structure of the stability theorem” combines “the nonlinear stability framework of Kenig, Ponce, and Vega (KPV)” with “a rigorous analysis of the residual minimization principle underlying physics-informed neural networks (PINNs).” The starting point is a PINNs/DNN approximation $u_{\mathrm{DNN},\#}$ “minimizing the residual functional”
\[
\mathcal E_k[u_{\mathrm{DNN},\#}] = \partial_t u_{\mathrm{DNN},\#} + \partial_x^3 u_{\mathrm{DNN},\#} + \partial_x(u_{\mathrm{DNN},\#}^k),
\]
“and other related functionals” (notably, one “minimizing the linear evolution”). The residual $\mathcal E_k[u_{\mathrm{DNN},\#}]$ is viewed as an “approximate solution” and also “as a nonlinear operator in suitable functional spaces,” which “will induce an associated functional setting for the corresponding solution.”

Using “the Duhamel formulation and dispersive linear estimates,” the difference $w=u-u_{\mathrm{DNN},\#}$ satisfies “an integral inequality controlled by the norms introduced in KPV.” With “small initial discrepancy and small residual (H2)--(H3), one obtains a Gronwall-type estimate” yielding the quantitative bound
\[
\|w\|_{Y_{k,s}}\lesssim C(A,\tilde A,L)\,\varepsilon.
\]
The space $Y_{k,s}$ is “adapted to each gKdV model, obtained from 
\begin{aligned}
    \|u\|_{Y_{2,s}(A)} &:= \|\partial_x u\|_{L_t^4L_x^{\infty}(A)} 
+ \|D_x^s\partial_x u\|_{L_x^{\infty}L_t^2(A)} \\
&~{} \quad + \|u\|_{L_x^2L_t^\infty(A)} 
+  \|u\|_{L_t^\infty H^s_x(A)};
\end{aligned} \label{eq:Y2s_norm}\\
& \begin{aligned}
\|u\|_{Y_{3,s}(A)} &:= \|\partial_x u\|_{L_x^\infty L_t^2(A)} 
+ \|u\|_{L_x^4L_t^\infty(A)} 
+ \|D^s_x \partial_x u\|_{L_x^\infty L_t^2(A)} \\
&~{} \quad + \|D_x^s u\|_{L_x^5L_t^{10}(A)}
+ \|\partial_x u\|_{L_x^{20}L_t^{5/2}(A)}; 
\end{aligned}
-\begin{aligned}
 \|u\|_{Y_{4,s}(A)} &:= \max_{t\in I} \|D^s_x w(t)\|_{L_x^2}+ \|\partial_x u\|_{L_t^\infty H_x^{s}(A)}
+ \|u\|_{L_x^{42/13}L_t^{21/4}(A)}
+ \|u\|_{L_x^{60/13}L_t^{15}(A)} \\
&~{} \quad + \|u\|_{W_x^{s,10/3}L_t^{30/7}(A)}
+ \|\partial_x u\|_{L_x^{\infty} L_t^2(A)}+ \|D^s_x\partial_x u\|_{L_x^{\infty} L_t^2(A)}; 
\end{aligned} \label{eq:Y4s_norm}\\
 &\|u\|_{Y_{5,s}(A)} := \|u\|_{L_x^5L_t^{10}(A)}. \label{eq:Y5s_norm}
\end{align}
are the norms that we will work in the paper. This norms \eqref{eq:Y2s_norm}-\eqref{eq:Y5s_norm} arises naturaly from the Kenig-Ponce-Vega theory. In addition we will say that $g \in Y_{k,s}(A)$ if $\|g\|_{Y_{k,s}(A)}<\infty$. Notice that under hypothesis (H1), the norms $\|\cdot\|_{Y_{k,s}}$ can be suitable approximated by a functional $\mathcal Y_{k,s,N,M}$ (cf. Definitions \eqref{eq:Y2s_appr}-\eqref{eq:Y5s_appr}).

\medskip

\noindent
{\bf Hypotheses on PINNs}.
 Let $u_{\text{DNN},\#}= u_{\text{DNN},\#} (t,x)$ be a smooth bounded function constructed by means of an algorithmic procedure (either SGD or any other ML optimization procedure) and realization of a suitable PINNs, in the following sense: under the framework \eqref{Loss1}, one has:
