They “find a diagram for each connected sum, together with a crossing change in each diagram, that results in a knot whose unknotting number we know is too low for the Unknotting Additivity Conjecture.” Concretely, they build “random diagrams of connected sums $K\#K^\prime$” (with $K\in\{3_1,4_1,5_1\}$ and $K^\prime$ chosen from knots with purported/known $u(K^\prime)=3$ or $2$), then do “random crossing changes” looking for a resulting knot $K^{\prime\prime}$ with “BJ-unknotting number, and hence also unknotting number, at most two.” Since “unknotting number changes by at most one under crossing change,” finding such a $K^{\prime\prime}$ forces $u(K\#K^\prime)\le 3$, yielding the desired strict inequality $u(K\#K^\prime)<u(K)+u(K^\prime)$. They note that such diagrams were found “dozens of times for each pair” and that the presented ones have “15 crossings,” with full details deferred to later.

