\begin{pmatrix}
-1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0\\
0 & -1 & 0 & 0 & b & 0\\
0 & 0 & -1 & 0 & c & e\\
1 & 0 & 0 & -1 & d & f\\
0 & b & c & d & -1 & g\\
0 & 0 & e & f & g & 2h-1
\end{pmatrix}.$$
Let $M_i$ be a $i\times i$ principal submatrix of $M$, and denote $\Delta_i = \det M_i$ the corresponding principal minors of $M$. One can easily find $$\Delta_1=-1,\,\, \Delta_2 =1, \,\, \Delta_3=-1,\,\, \Delta_4=0,\,\, \Delta_5=d^2,\,\, \Delta_6 = \det M.$$
 We are interested in case $d\neq0$, i.e. $M_5$ is non-singular. Provided that $b^2+c^2+d^2=1$, the characteristic polynomial of $M_5$ is given by:
$$\chi(M_5; \lambda) = -(\lambda+1)(\lambda^2+2\lambda-d)(\lambda^2+2\lambda+d).$$
The eigenvalues of $M_5$ are given by $-1$, $-1\pm\sqrt{1+d}$, $-1 \pm \sqrt{1-d}$,
hence $M_5$ has four negative and one positive root. Thus, by Cauchy interlacing theorem, $M_6 = M$ has at least four negative roots. This in turn means that $P(0; \lambda)$ has at least four roots smaller than $\frac{1}{2}$.
\par Therefore, $\sqrt{\rho_+}$ is at least the second largest root of $P(0; \lambda)$. As $P(0; \lambda)$ has positive leading coefficient, there is then a simple criterion: $\sqrt{\rho_+}$ is the largest root if and only if $P'(0; \sqrt{\rho_+})$ is positive. Now, observe that 

{\color{magenta}
$$P'(0;\lambda)
=
6\lambda^{5}
-5h\,\lambda^{4}
+(h^{2}-3)\lambda^{3}
+\frac{3}{2}h\,\lambda^{2}
+\frac{3-d^{2}-f^{2}-g^{2}-2h^{2}}{8}\,\lambda
+\frac{h(d^{2}-1)-dfg}{16},$$
$$P'(0;\sqrt{\rho_+})
=
-\frac{h}{4}\,x(x+1)
+\frac{\sqrt{1+x}}{16}\Bigl(3x^{2}+2h^{2}x+h^{2}+e^{2}-d^{2}-1\Bigr)
$$}

