\medskip
A \textit{rank metric code} is a subset $\mathcal C$ of the $\F_q$-vector space $\F_q^{m \times n}$ of the matrices with entries over $\F_q$ containing at least two elements and equipped with the \textit{rank distance}, defined as
$$
d(A,B)=\mathrm{rk}(A-B) \,\,\, \mathrm{for \,\, any} \,\,\, A,B \in \F_{q}^{m \times n}.
$$
The \textit{minimum distance} $d(\mathcal C)$ of $\mathcal C$ is defined as
$$
d=d(\mathcal C)=\min_{\underset{A \neq B}{A,B \in \mathcal C}} d(A,B).
$$
The code $\mathcal C$ will be called a \textit{rank metric code}, or for short \textit{RM code} with parameters $(m,n,q;d)$. An \textit{RM code} is \textit{linear} if it is an $\F_q$-subspace of $\F_{q}^{m \times n}$. It is well known that the size of a rank metric code satisfies the \textit{Singleton-like bound} \cite[Theorem 5.4]{Singleton}, i.e.,
$$
|\mathcal C| \leq q^{\max\{m,n\}(\min\{m,n\}-d+1)}.
$$
When this bound is attained, the code $\mathcal C$ is called a \textit{maximum rank distance} code, or shortly \textit{MRD} code. Two linear rank metric codes $\mathcal C, \mathcal C' \subseteq \F_{q}^{m \times n}$ are \textit{equivalent} if there exist $A \in \mathrm{GL}(m,q)$, $B \in \mathrm{GL}(n,q)$ and $\rho \in \mathrm{Aut}(\F_q)$ such that
$$
\mathcal C'=A \mathcal C^{\rho} B:= \{ AC^{\rho}B: C \in \mathcal C \}.
$$
Since there is an isomorphism between elements of $\tilde{\mathcal L}_{n,q,s}$ and $\mathrm{End}_{\F_q}(\F_{q^n})$, a rank metric code made up of $n \times n$ matrices can be seen as a subset of $\tilde{\mathcal L}_{n,q,s}$. In this setting, the minimum distance of $\mathcal C \subseteq \tilde{\mathcal L}_{n,q,s}$ is defined as
$$
\min_{\underset{f,g \in \mathcal C}{f \neq g}} \dim_{\F_q} \mathrm{im} ((f - g)(x)),
$$
and the equivalence between $\mathcal C, \mathcal C' \subseteq \tilde{\mathcal L}_{n,q,s}$ can be stated as follows: there are $g,h \in \tilde{\mathcal L}_{n,q,s}$ permutation polynomials and $\rho \in \mathrm{Aut}(\F_q)$ such that
$$
\mathcal C'=g \circ \mathcal C^{\rho} \circ h:=\{ g \circ f^{\rho} \circ h : f \in \mathcal C \},
$$
where the automorphism $\rho$ acts only on the coefficients of the polynomial $f$. In general, it is rather difficult to determine whether two rank metric codes are equivalent or not. For this reason, it is useful to introduce the concepts of \textit{left} and \textit{right idealizers} of a rank metric code $\mathcal C \subseteq \tilde{\mathcal L}_{n,q,s}$, that is
$$
I_{L}(\mathcal C)=\{ g \in \tilde{\mathcal L}_{n,q,s} \colon \, g \circ f \in \mathcal C \quad \forall f \in \mathcal C \}
$$
and 
$$I_{R}(\mathcal C)=\{ h \in \tilde{\mathcal L}_{n,q,s} \colon \, f \circ h \in \mathcal C \quad \forall f \in \mathcal C \},
$$
respectively. As shown in \cite[Proposition 4.1]{LTZ}, left and right idealizers are invariant under equivalence. Moreover, if $\mathcal C$ is a linear MRD code, they are isomorphic to subfields of $\F_{q^n}$; see \cite{survey}.

In literature, up to now, there are only three families of maximum scattered linear sets of the projective line $\PG(1,q^n)$ for infinitely many $n$ and $q$:
\begin{itemize}
 \item [$(i)$] $f_{1,s}:=X^{q^s}$, known as \textit{pseudoregulus type}, where $1 \leq s \leq n-1$ and $\gcd(s,n) = 1$, see \cite{BlockLaw}. In this case we have:
 $$
 G_{f_{1,s}}^{\circ}=\left\{ \begin{pmatrix} \alpha & 0 \\ 0 & \alpha^{q^s} \end{pmatrix} : \alpha \in \F_{q^n}\right\} \,\,\, \mathrm{and} \,\,\, I_R(\mathcal{C}_{f_{1,s}})=\{ \alpha X : \alpha \in \F_{q^n} \},
 $$
 see \cite{Cjabok}.
 \item [$(ii)$] $f_{2,s}:=X^{q^s} + \delta X^{q^{s(n-1)}}$, known as \textit{Lunardon-Polverino (LP) type}, where $n \geq 4$, $\mathrm{N}_{q^n/q}(\delta)\notin \{0,1\} $, $1 \leq s \leq n-1$ and $\gcd(s,n) = 1$, see \cite{LunPol,SheMRD}. Then,
 $$
 G_{f_{2,s}}^{\circ}=\left\{ \begin{pmatrix} \alpha & 0 \\ 0 & \alpha^{q^s} \end{pmatrix} : \alpha \in \F_{q^{\gcd(2,n)}}\right\} \,\,\, \mathrm{and} \,\,\, I_R(\mathcal{C}_{f_{2,s}})=\{ \alpha X : \alpha \in \F_{q^{\gcd(2,n)}} \},
 $$
 see \cite{translation, Cjabok}.
 \item [$(iii)$] 
 \begin{equation}\label{quad}
 \psi_{m,h,s}:= m \left (X^{q^s}-h^{1-q^{s(t+1)}}X^{q^{s(t+1)}} \right )+X^{q^{s(t-1)}}+h^{1-q^{s(2t-1)}}X^{q^{s(2t-1)}} \in \tilde{\mathcal{L}}_{n,q,s}, 
 \end{equation}
 where $q$ is an odd prime power, $t \geq 3$, $n=2t$, $\gcd(s,n)=1$, and $(m,h) \in \F_{q^t} \times \F_{q^{2t}}$ are such that 
 \begin{itemize}
 \item $m=1$ and $h \in \F_{q^t}$ with $h^2=-1$, see \cite{Bartoli_Zanella_Zullo,longobardi_zanella, neri_santonastaso_zullo,Zanella-Zullo}. Note that $h^2=-1$ implies that $h \in \F_{q^{\gcd(t,2)}}$. So, if $t$ is odd, $q \equiv 1 \pmod 4$.

