expanded_sketch:
To prove the main theorem, the argument “partially follows the lines” of Bellettini–Mantegazza–Novaga, “Convergence for regularized curvature flow of closed immersed curves” (2004), with the novelty here being “accounting for boundary terms and for the tangential component of the velocity” \(\lambda_\eps:=\langle \partial_t \gamma_\eps, \tau_{\gamma_\eps}\rangle\).

The “crucial point” is to obtain “\(\varepsilon\)-independent integral estimates of the curvature and all its derivatives.” Concretely, “by an ODE's argument,” since all flows \((0<\varepsilon\le 1)\) start from the same smooth initial curve, for any fixed \(j\in\mathbb N\) there is a “common positive interval of time” on which all \(\|\partial_s^i\kappa\|_{L^2}\) for \(i\in\{0,\dots,j\}\) are “equibounded.” These uniform bounds “allow us to get compactness and \(C^\infty\) convergence to the curvature flow as \(\varepsilon\to 0^+\).”

It is also emphasized that the convergence result “holds up to \(\Tsingmcf\), and not only up to \(\Tmax\)” (as defined in the following auxiliary result.

