\label{sec:intro}

\subsection{What is known}
\label{sec:intro_what_is_known}

Compact generation of derived categories of quasi-coherent sheaves on algebraic stacks plays a central role in modern algebraic geometry. In many cases of interest, the triangulated category $D_{\operatorname{qc}}$ is singly compactly generated. That is, there exists a single compact object whose shifts, cones, and small coproducts generate $D_{\operatorname{qc}}$.

For quasi-compact quasi-separated schemes, $D_{\operatorname{qc}}$ being singly compactly generated was established by Bondal and Van den Bergh \cite[Theorem 3.1.1]{Bondal/VandenBergh:2003}. Extensions to various classes of Deligne--Mumford and algebraic stacks—typically in characteristic zero or under resolution property assumptions—were obtained in \cite[Corollary 5.2]{Toen:2012}, \cite[proof of Proposition 5.5]{Krishna:2009}, and \cite[\S 3.3]{Ben-Zvi/Francis/Nadler:2010}.

A recent advance was made by Hall and Rydh, who proved that $D_{\operatorname{qc}}$ is singly compactly generated for any quasi-compact quasi-separated algebraic stack with quasi-finite and separated diagonal \cite[Theorem A]{Hall/Rydh:2017a}. In that work, Hall and Rydh introduced the $\beta$-Thomason condition, where $\beta$ is a cardinal. This requires not only compact generation of $D_{\operatorname{qc}}$ by a collection of cardinality $\leq \beta$, but also the existence of perfect complexes with prescribed support. Note that the $1$-Thomason condition is stronger than $D_{\operatorname{qc}}$ being singly compactly generated.

\subsection{Why it matters}
\label{sec:intro_why_it_matters}

Before stating our results, we briefly explain the importance of the existence of compact generators for $D_{\operatorname{qc}}$. Philosophically, $D_{\operatorname{qc}}$ and its subcategories encode homological information that is expected to reflect the geometry of the underlying algebraic stack. In particular, a substantial body of work has been developed around compact objects (see e.g.\ \cite{Neeman:1992,Neeman:1996}). This provides a manageable framework for extracting and organizing such information. These ideas have played a central role in resolving conjectures \cite{Hall/Rydh:2017b, Neeman:2021a, Neeman:2022} and in the study of singularity theory for schemes (see e.g.\ \cite{Lank/Venkatesh:2024}). They have also been essential in classification results for triangulated and derived categories (see e.g.\ \cite{Hall:2016, Lank:2025}) and in 
$K$-theoretic applications (see e.g.\ \cite{Joshua:2002a, Joshua:2002b, Toen:1999, Krishna:2009}).

\subsection{What is done}
\label{sec:intro_what_is_done}

The main result of this paper is the following.

