expanded_sketch:
To prove the main theorem, we proceed as follows. First, show that the particular form of the Green function is equivalent to the following equation for the mean curvature of $M$:
\begin{align} \label{eq:surface2}
    \frac{2(\langle y, \nu \rangle)^2}{|y|^4}- H \frac{\langle y,\nu \rangle }{|y|^2}+\frac{1}{2}=0 \quad, x\in M\setminus\{0\}.
\end{align}
Second, using this equation, prove that $M$ is star-shaped, so it can be viewed as a radial graph over $\mathbb{S}^2_{+}$. Then convert the equation above into the following equation for the radial function $\rho$:
\begin{align} \label{eq: radial}
\operatorname{div}(\frac{\nabla \rho}{\sqrt{1+|\nabla \rho|^2}})=-\frac{1}{2} e^{2\rho}\sqrt{1+|\nabla \rho|^2}.
\end{align}
This is a minimal-surface-type equation on $\mathbb{S}^2_{+}$ with Dirichlet boundary condition $\rho=-\infty$. Finally, apply the moving plane ("indeed, rotating circle") method to show any solution of this radial equation must be rotationally symmetric, from which the main conclusion follows.

